  Quick, inline, no custom class needed
  But: less structured than @ExceptionHandler
```

### How does @ExceptionHandler compare to ResponseStatusException?

| Feature | @ExceptionHandler | ResponseStatusException |
|---|---|---|
| Location | Centralized in @ControllerAdvice | Inline in controller/service |
| Response body | Full custom ErrorResponse DTO | Spring's default error body |
| Reusability | One handler for all controllers | Per-throw-site |
| Custom exception class | Required (TradeNotFoundException) | Not required |
| Best for | Production APIs with consistent format | Quick prototypes, simple cases |

### What are common mistakes in Spring exception handling?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class SpringExceptionMistakes {

    // WRONG: try-catch in every controller method
    // @GetMapping("/{id}")
    // public ResponseEntity<Trade> getTrade(@PathVariable String id) {
    //     try {
    //         return ResponseEntity.ok(tradeService.findById(id));
    //     } catch (TradeNotFoundException e) {
    //         return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
    //     } catch (Exception e) {
    //         return ResponseEntity.internalServerError().build();
    //     }
    // }
    // 200 try-catch blocks across 15 controllers. Inconsistent.

    // CORRECT: let exceptions propagate to @ControllerAdvice

    // WRONG: exposing internal details in error response
    // return new ErrorResponse("ERROR", exception.toString(), 500);
    // Leaks: class names, SQL queries, stack traces

    // CORRECT: generic message for 500 errors
    // return new ErrorResponse("INTERNAL_ERROR", "Something went wrong", 500);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Common mistakes:");
        System.out.println("1. try-catch in every controller method");
        System.out.println("2. Exposing SQL errors to API clients");
        System.out.println("3. Missing catch-all handler for Exception.class");
        System.out.println("4. Not logging the full exception in catch-all");
    }
}
```

